<p>The table is a general guide. The cleaning method, the materials affected and the policy language all shape what the insurer should include.</p>

<h2>What a Complete Smoke Damage Claim Looks At</h2>
<h3>The building</h3>
<ul>
<li>Walls and ceilings in every affected room, not just the room of origin</li>
<li>Cabinet interiors, closets, attic framing and insulation</li>
<li>Windows, blinds, light fixtures and ceiling fans</li>
<li>Whether surfaces need cleaning only, cleaning plus odor-sealing primer and paint, or replacement</li>
</ul>
<h3>The HVAC system</h3>
<p>Return air grilles, filters, the evaporator coil and ductwork can carry residue into every room. Flex duct common in Port Arthur attics can be difficult to clean, and replacement is sometimes the practical answer. Ask that the system be inspected rather than assumed clean.</p>
<h3>Contents</h3>
<p>Clothing, bedding, upholstered furniture, rugs, electronics and kitchen items may all be affected. Some can be professionally cleaned; others cannot be restored. A room-by-room inventory with photos is the backbone of the contents portion of the claim. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> walks through what to capture.</p>
<h3>Living expenses and business income</h3>
<p>If the home is not livable during cleaning, additional living expense coverage may apply. For a business, smoke that forces a closure can raise <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">business interruption</a> questions under a commercial policy.</p>

